    {
      "assumption": "Impairment charges and ECL provisioning decline meaningfully from the elevated FY22 level as provision coverage ratio has crossed 82.68%, implying the worst of the NPA recognition cycle is behind IFCI.",
      "failure_scenario": "Macro stress or a borrower-specific contagion event triggers fresh Stage-2 to Stage-3 migrations in IFCI's residual loan book, requiring additional provisions well above the forecast glide path; furthermore, regulatory scrutiny or an RBI/government audit forces re-classification of under-reported NPAs, restarting the provisioning cycle and keeping losses elevated well beyond FY25.",
      "risk": "High"
    },
    {
      "assumption": "Government capital infusion is not assumed within the 3-year horizon, yet the forecast implicitly relies on IFCI continuing to operate as a going concern despite a CRAR of -64.85% and deeply negative CET1 capital.",
      "failure_scenario": "Absence of timely government recapitalisation leads regulators or lenders to impose funding restrictions, accelerating liability run-off, triggering rating downgrades, and raising the cost of existing borrowings; this could cause a liquidity crunch that forces asset fire-sales at distressed prices, rendering revenue and loss projections materially worse than forecast and potentially calling into question IFCI's ability to continue as a going concern.",
      "risk": "High"
    }
